According to the repository's general rules, utility functions (such as sorting, filtering, or mapping helpers) must be robust against invalid or missing inputs by returning a safe default (e.g., an empty object or array) to fail closed gracefully instead of throwing a `TypeError`.

Currently, `sortedObject` directly calls `Object.entries(entries)` without checking if `entries` is a valid object. If `entries` is `null` or `undefined`, this will throw a `TypeError` and crash the process.

Please add a guard to return a safe default (e.g., `{}`) if `entries` is not a valid object.

```suggestion
function sortedObject(entries) {
  if (entries === null || entries === undefined || typeof entries !== 'object') return {};
  return Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(entries).sort(([left], [right]) => (
    compareByteOrder(left, right)
  )));
}
```

